              <h3>Functional and non-functional testing</h3>
<p>So far you have learnt about different types, levels and methods of coding and we have talked about functional and non-functional testing. Lets take a look at the differences in more detail and when you would use the different methods.</p>

              <!-- sections.njk VIDEO! -->
              
              
              
      <div class="l-video-container">
        <video poster="~/images/svg/poster.svg" controls preload="metadata" aria-labelledby="watch" width="1000"
          data-base="cp3_u4_s1_p2_functional_testing" 
          data-ext=".mp4" 
          data-multi="false"
          data-trans="cp3_u4_s1_p2_functional_testing undefined">
          <source src="https://kpcontent.blob.core.windows.net/$web/resources/v2l/720/cp3_u4_s1_p2_functional_testing.mp4" type="video/mp4">
          <track default label="English" kind="captions" srclang="en" src="~/videos/captions/vtt/cp3_u4_s1_p2_functional_testing.vtt">
          <p>Sorry, your browser doesn't support embedded videos</p>
        </video>
      </div>
      
          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p>Hi, and welcome to the short video where we will look at functional testing and non-functional testing. So in this session, we've already looked at different types of testing and when that testing is carried out, whether it be as part of the software development life cycle, or whether it be carried out black box testing or white box testing.</p>
<p>But, so what is functional testing and non-functional testing in relation to that? So what's the difference between.</p>
<p>So the types of functional testing we have, unit testing, integration testing, system testing, interface testing, smoke testing, sanity testing, regression testing, and acceptance testing. There are other elements, there may be other different types of tests that were carried out, but these are the most common that you will find of functional testing types.</p>
<p>So this is basically designed to test the requirements or specifications, so all the technical details, the data manipulation processing, and the various functionalities that you will have within an application or a piece of software. But you've also got other testing, which is non-functional testing.</p>
<p>So what do we mean when we talk about non-functional testing? So the non-functional testing element of any testing process relates to the software testing, where it's looking at non-functional aspects, performance, usability, reliability, so stress what happens to the system when it's under load. So the different types of testing that you have are documentation testing, which is an important element within the software development, life cycle, performance testing, so how does the actual application perform? Does it perform as expected? Usability, does it meet the usability standards for the application? What is expected, security testing, which is a very important element within any software application. Load testing, so what happens when the application is put under load, which we've already talked about on a previous page, and volume testing.</p>
<p>So what's the difference between the two? So if we have a look at this table, you can think about it in relation to the parameter, so what is the objective? What is the objective of functional testing as opposed to non-functional testing? So when you look at the objective of functional testing, it's mainly carried out to verify and validate that the software, such as login functions, work as expected, whereas the non-functional testing helps in verify and validate the performance of the software. So if you're doing a functional test, does it work? When somebody inputs an expected piece of information, does the software respond appropriately, and then you've also got the key focus area, so functional testing concentrates on the user requirements.</p>
<p>So what, what the system should do under any given situation and the processes that will take place, whereas the non-functional testing concentrates on the user expectations. So how, like how is the system built? How does it interact with the user and so on, and then ease of use easy to execute black box test cases.</p>
<p>So, you know, functional testing is easy to execute and it's quite straightforward you don't need to know anything under the underlying code or anything like that, and it's all about the um, does it perform as expected? Non-functional testing is easy to execute white box test cases, and again, this is looking at the internal structure and implementation process, and so you can see what's happening inside the box. Functionality um, so the functional testing helps describe what the system should do, whereas the non-functional, it describes how the system should work. So the first one is what it should do. How does it work. So the functional testing provides the answers to, to what the functions in the system are doing, whereas non-functional answers to how the questions in the system and execution.</p>
<p>So functional testing is executed before the non-functional testing. So it always takes place before the non-functional testing, because this defines the the, the testing and the processes that are going to be executed first, where the non-functional only takes place after the execution has happened. So as you can see, that's just a brief overview of the differences.</p>
<p>So in your assessment you'll be asked to look at what's the difference between functional and non-functional. So it may be useful to identify the different testing methods and use those as examples to illustrate your answer. So, unit testing, what kind of testing is that and why is that done? What's the difference between that and a, an element of nonfunctional testing?</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
